  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nAn upper level low pressure system will lift northeast from the\nCentral Plains across the upper Mississippi Valley and western Great\nLakes tonight into Wednesday. As it does, an embedded mid level \nshort wave rotating around the base of the low will lift northeast \ninto the Ohio Valley. In response to this - a 45-50 KT 8H low level \njet will shift east across ILN/s area late tonight into Wednesday. \n\nFavorable moisture transport will advect into our area with PWATs \nincreasing to around 1.5 inches. This leads to widespread showers \ndeveloping later tonight across ILN/s southwest/west and then \noverspreading the rest of our area through Wednesday morning. \n\nShowers will the continue through the afternoon before tapering off \nfrom the west. Very marginal instability develops on Wednesday,\nso a a few embedded thunderstorms will be possible, especially \nacross southern portions of our area. Deep layer shear will be \nstrong, but any severe threat should be limited by the lack of better\ninstabilities. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible given the \nincreasing moisture, with the heaviest rainfall amounts expected \nmainly along and south of the Ohio River. Storm total amounts of 1 to\n1.5 inches will be possible in spots across our southwest, but given\nthe recent dry stretch of weather, the overall flood threat will \nremain low.\n\n\nKey message 2) \n\nA mid and upper level trof will develop into a cut off low over the \nGreat Lakes this weekend. A secondary surface cold front will drop \nthru the area Friday - leading to a chance for showers and cooler \ntemperatures. This low will be followed by a northwest flow pattern\naloft into early next week. A series of mid level short waves \ndropping down across the Great Lakes will likely lead to occasional \nlower end chances for showers. Temperatures will trend back to \nseasonable readings with highs over the weekend and into early next \nweek generally in the 55 to 60 degree range.",
